Environment & Setup: The Conda Guide

To ensure your research engine runs predictably, you must isolate your workspace. Think of a Virtual Environment as a sealed clean room where you define exactly which tools are on the shelves .

Step 1: Install your Distributor
Before creating an environment, you need a distribution manager.

Anaconda: The "landlord" of your building. It comes pre-packaged with a vast library of data science tools.

Miniforge/Miniconda: A lighter, "bare-bones" version for those who want to save disk space and system resources.

Step 2: Create the "Workshop"
Open your terminal (or Anaconda Prompt) and run the following command to create a clean, isolated room. We recommend using Python 3.11 or 3.12 for maximum compatibility with modern LLM libraries.

Bash
# Create an environment named 'fortress'
conda create -n fortress python=3.11

Step 3: Activate the Environment
You must "enter" the room before you can start working.

Bash
conda activate fortress
Step 4: Install the Essentials
Once inside, use pip to install the core "toolbox" required for this repository.

Bash
pip install pandas streamlit openai tavily-python python-dotenv

The Setup Hierarchy
Always remember the "Workforce Hierarchy" to prevent library conflicts :

Distributor: Anaconda (The Landlord).

Environment: Your 'fortress' workshop (The Locked Room).

Interface: VS Code or Jupyter (The Workbench).

Pro-Tip: Always launch your code editor (like VS Code) from within the activated environment to ensure the scripts "see" the libraries you've installed.
